menu "CSI sniffer configuration"

    config CSI_SNIFFER_CHANNEL
        int "Channel to park on"
        range 1 177
        default 36
        help
            2.4 GHz: 1-14.
            5 GHz:   36-64, 100-144, 149-177, in steps of 4.

            36 is the default because it is a non-DFS 5 GHz channel that most
            dual-band APs use out of the box, and 5 GHz is the reason to pick a
            C5 over a C6 in the first place. Use 1 or 6 or 11 if the traffic you
            care about is on 2.4 GHz.

            Channels 52-144 are DFS. A C5 in promiscuous mode only listens, so
            receiving there is fine, but many APs avoid them and you may see no
            traffic at all.

    config CSI_SNIFFER_PRINT_CSV
        bool "Print one CSV line per record"
        default y
        help
            Turn this off to keep only the periodic summary. Useful when you
            want to check that CSI is flowing without drowning the console.

    config CSI_SNIFFER_DUMP_ACK
        bool "Also produce CSI for received 802.11 ACK frames"
        default y
        help
            ACKs are short, frequent and sent at a legacy rate, which makes them
            a dense and very regular CSI source. This maps to the dump_ack_en
            field of wifi_csi_acquire_config_t.

    config CSI_SNIFFER_FORCE_LLTF
        bool "Report L-LTF instead of HT/VHT/HE-LTF"
        default n
        help
            This maps to acquire_csi_force_lltf. On the C5 an HT/VHT/HE frame
            carries two LTF sequences and you get exactly one of them - and the
            choice silently changes the buffer format as well:

              n (default) - HT/VHT/HE-LTF. 8-bit int8 pairs, two bytes per tone.
                            245 tones in a 490-byte record for an HE20 frame,
                            the most frequency resolution available.
              y           - L-LTF only, always 106 bytes, the same shape no
                            matter which PHY the frame used. But L-LTF defaults
                            to 12-BIT packing on this chip: four bytes per tone,
                            26 tones, not 53.

            The 12-bit behaviour is undocumented - ESP-IDF's CSI page still says
            two bytes per sub-carrier - and reading it as int8 pairs produces
            data that "parses cleanly and plots plausibly" while being wrong.
            The shared component decodes both and tags each record in the
            "packing" CSV column.

    config CSI_SNIFFER_VAL_SCALE
        int "CSI value scaling (val_scale_cfg)"
        range 0 8
        default 0
        help
            Scales the reported I/Q values. The C5 accepts 0-8; the C6 field is
            only two bits wide and stops at 3.

            ESP-IDF documents nothing about this field except its range - not
            what the values mean, and not whether 0 is special. So treat it
            empirically: leave it at 0, look at the printed values, and only
            change it if they clip at +/-127 or sit down in the noise.

    config CSI_SNIFFER_QUEUE_LEN
        int "CSI records buffered between the Wi-Fi task and the printer"
        range 4 64
        default 16
        help
            Each slot is roughly 600 bytes. Raise it if the summary line reports
            drops, lower it if you are short of RAM. Records are dropped rather
            than blocking, because blocking in the CSI callback stalls the whole
            Wi-Fi stack.

endmenu
